08. How to publish and protect the pricing
The rate card is only profitable when the contract describes what is included. These policies should appear on the pricing
page, proposal template or service terms.
Policy Recommended wording / operating rule
Payment terms Websites: 50% to schedule, 30% after design approval, 20% before launch/hand-over. Smaller fixed
tasks: 100% upfront. Monthly plans: billed in advance.
Scope control List page count, revision rounds, forms, integrations, products, languages, locations, users, support
hours and included edits. Any change outside scope is quoted before work begins.
Client materials Client supplies accurate business information, images, legal policies, product details, access
permissions and approvals. Copywriting, photography and translation are separate unless included.
Third-party charges Domains, hosting, premium themes/plugins, email, CRM, phone numbers, API usage, AI minutes,
WhatsApp messages, software seats and advertising spend are billed separately or explicitly itemised.
Ownership and access Use client-owned domains and platform accounts. Transfer agreed website files, admin access and
documentation after final payment. Keep administrator credentials out of informal chat where possible.
SEO expectations No guaranteed rankings, leads, revenue or inclusion in AI-generated answers. Report measurable work
and agreed indicators, and explain that search engines control rankings.
Support boundaries Define business hours, response target, included support hours and urgent/out-of-hours rates. A
response target is not a guarantee of resolution time.
Taxes and currency List prices before applicable tax unless the tax treatment is confirmed. Quote in the client's local
currency or state USD clearly.
CCTV and onsite work Onsite work is dependent on local partner availability and a site survey. Hardware, cabling, permits,
travel, electrical work and storage subscriptions are itemised.
AI safety and privacy Use client-approved knowledge sources, clear human handoff, access controls and retention settings.
The client is responsible for required notices, lawful recording consent and privacy disclosures in its
jurisdiction.
